WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:05.520
Movie-watching can be an enriching communal
experience, but some films are better enjoyed

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.520
solo.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.929
Contemplative dramedies, slow-burning thrillers,
dialogue-free dramas — certain films are

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:14.289
best absorbed all by yourself, with zero distractions.

00:00:14.289 --> 00:00:15.809
For example ...

00:00:15.809 --> 00:00:17.310
All Is Lost

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:21.710
Robert Redford's ability to grip audiences
with a compelling performance has never waned

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:23.060
throughout his long career.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:28.680
That gift is particularly evident in J.C.
Chandor's All Is Lost, an acclaimed 2013 drama

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:32.710
in which Redford's unnamed sailor endures
a litany of increasingly life-threatening

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.370
mishaps during a solo ocean voyage.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.100
Watching this film alone is a transformative
experience.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.910
There's only one character and he hardly ever
speaks, which makes sounds that ordinarily

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.440
might not be noticed all the more intriguing.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.070
With most of the sound coming from the main
character shuffling around, what would normally

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:52.420
be an ordinary background detail becomes a
key factor in the film's immersion.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.840
Every detail in All Is Lost requires your
full attention.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:57.840
Gravity

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.320
Alfonso Cuarón's hit 2013 space drama Gravity
is a film that filled theaters for weeks—but

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.500
in order to truly exert its devastating impact,
it needs to be seen alone.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.620
For one, the filmmakers do an excellent job
of projecting panic onto the audience after

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.220
Sandra Bullock's character is marooned in
space.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.780
Fear is alleviated somewhat in a group setting,
so truly putting yourself in her position

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.820
requires watching the film alone.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:24.780
Whether or not there's sound in space is up
for debate, but Cuarón's command of Gravity's

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.750
soundtrack is the second reason you should
watch it by yourself—and with the volume

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.750
high.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.700
It's the type of film in which the intensity
continuously builds, amplified by the skillful

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.770
use of sound.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.890
Even the crescendo in the score allows audiences
to feel the emotional weight of Bullock's

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.460
character's struggle to reach Earth—and
survive.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.130
It's a thrillride best experienced alone,
with nothing to distract you.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:47.470
Into the Wild

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:51.750
Based on the novel of the same name by Jon
Krakauer, Into the Wild tells the true story

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.310
of Christopher McCandless, a recent college
grad who braved the Alaskan wilderness in

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.810
the early '90s and sadly passed away a few
months into his trek.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.340
Into the Wild's judgment-free dramatization
of McCandless' doomed pilgrimage offers a

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:08.170
perfectly introspective example of a movie
that makes the view think about what they

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:09.720
would do in a similar situation.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:14.069
Who knows, maybe you'll be inspired to abandon
materialism and go on a solo adventure of

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:15.950
your own in the Alaskan wilderness.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.080
It's an incredibly dangerous proposition,
but you wouldn't be the first.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.080
The Fountain

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.830
Darren Aronofsky's filmography is studded
with Biblical symbolism, and The Fountain,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.870
starring Hugh Jackman and Rachel Weisz as
star-crossed lovers, offers a particularly

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.269
absorbing example.

00:02:31.269 --> 00:02:35.480
The film is a visual spectacle, to say the
least, but its three storylines are just as

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.629
finely crafted—and easy to lose track of
if you aren't paying close attention.

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:43.730
Lose concentration and you could easily miss
details crucial to grasping the story as a

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:44.730
whole.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.340
The Fountain is an exceptionally emotional
story, but watching it unfold with a group

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.780
of friends might very well take you out of
the moment.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:53.859
The Assassination of Jesse James by the Coward
Robert Ford

00:02:53.859 --> 00:02:58.180
It may be slow at times—okay, most of the
time—but this dramatization of the titular

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:02.920
infamous killing never stops building in intensity,
due in large part to the stylized directing

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.430
style of Andrew Dominik and the masterful
eye of cinematographer Roger Deakins.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.299
The train robbery scene, in particular, is
an excellent example of just how hypnotic

00:03:11.299 --> 00:03:12.790
cinematography can be.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.840
Do yourself a favor and tune out chatter during
a masterpiece like The Assassination of Jesse

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.840
James by the Coward Robert Ford.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.450
Lost in Translation

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.810
Featuring one of Bill Murray's greatest performances,
along with exceptional work from Scarlett

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:30.319
Johansson, Lost in Translation earned Sofia
Coppola the third Best Director nomination

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:32.370
offered to a woman in Oscars history.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.519
It isn't that taking in Lost in Translation
with a crowd will make you lose sight of the

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:40.379
story—there isn't much of a plot—but the
self-reflection the movie motivates stands

00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:41.700
to be lost in a group.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:45.160
Like Johansson's character learns in that
unforgettable final scene, there are some

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:49.140
secrets you only learn when you're leaning
in close enough to hear a whisper.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.340
Any of Hayao Miyazaki's films

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.109
Legendary Japanese filmmaker Hayao Miyazaki
has done more for animation than many casual

00:03:56.109 --> 00:03:57.859
filmgoers might realize.

00:03:57.859 --> 00:04:01.919
His movies may not be as well-known to the
general public as Disney's library of hits,

00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:06.749
but from The Castle of Cagliostro to The Wind
Rises, Miyazaki and his Studio Ghibli have

00:04:06.749 --> 00:04:11.280
expanded the animation genre to make room
for sophisticated storytelling and exceptionally

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:12.280
beautiful visuals.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.400
They aren't event movies, though, and they
aren't made for watching with a group of friends.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.850
Tackling thought-provoking themes in their
own distinctive style, Miyazaki movies demand

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.880
the type of emotional room you can only get
solo.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.690
No Country for Old Men

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:29.720
Based on the Cormac McCarthy novel of the
same name, this modern Western thriller won

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.430
Best Picture and Best Director at the Oscars.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:36.150
It's considered one of the greatest films
of the century, as well as the Coen brothers'

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.350
masterpiece…and that's saying something.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:42.460
If you're unfamiliar with the form, Westerns
can be slow, punctuated with intermittent

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.370
bursts of heightened action, and No Country
for Old Men is a prime example.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.950
The tension, suspense, and thrills never stop
building, yet the film as a whole is eerily

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:51.950
quiet.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.950
If you're forced to deal with distractions
during the movie, you'll be taken right out

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.060
of the story—breaking its spell and ruining
the experience altogether.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:04:59.060
"Yes sir.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.100
I got it under control."

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:05.540
Thanks for watching!

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.920
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.169
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

